title The hand the watcher asked for
prompt Keaton -- restart the fleet and watch for my session exit; our first Claude plan expired intentionally and I logged into the other one
think Checked health before restarting anything, because killing a loop mid-lap loses the work in flight. Two of seven were alive; five had been dark about five hours by their own output mtimes.
obs THE WATCHER HAD ALREADY DONE ITS JOB AND SAID SO. It re-armed each dead seat four times, watched each die inside 180s, then stopped and printed that a hand is needed -- the loop's own quickfail law one level up, working exactly as written rather than hammering a wall all night.
obs KEATON'S WORD EXPLAINED THE WALL. The first Claude plan expired on purpose and he logged into the other; the deaths cluster where that account ran out, which is why a hand and not a smarter watcher was the answer.
obs ARMED ONE SEAT FIRST rather than five, and watched it past the 180s settle window before trusting the rest. Copal survived and was visibly working, so the other four followed and all seven stood after 200s.
obs RESTARTED THE WATCHER so its burned arm counters reset. A watcher that had given up on five seats would keep believing they need a hand, and the counter lives in the process rather than on disk.
obs I NEARLY REPORTED TWO FAULTS THAT WERE MINE. The count read 14 of 7 because my loop matched both the shell and its subshell per tree; and the clock looked to have jumped twelve hours, which is simply the time that passed between turns while Keaton was away. Peers committed through that gap at 11:46 and 12:06, which is what settles it.
obs Stopping the watcher resolved its tree through /proc first and would have refused a process outside this checkout, which is the bounded form the baton asks for rather than a pattern kill.
loom fleet peers_alive_before=2 armed=5 peers_alive_after=7 settle_seconds=200 watcher=1
loom watcher arm_max=3 settle=180 skip=none gave_up_seats=5
rota none -- a fleet restart on Keaton's word, no rota this lap
file none in the tree; the work was five loops, one watcher, and this record
scope five dead seats armed after a single-seat trial, the watcher restarted with fresh counters, incense left free to arm on exit
status GREEN -- seven peer loops alive past the quickfail window, one watcher running, no sentinel holding incense
recommend check in (Keaton) -- sleep; incense arms itself when this pane closes and the handoff is the next session's first read
